 ## Overview
-The ray-box intersection method determines whether a ray intersects an axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) by treating the box as the intersection of three pairs of parallel planes, known as slabs [S1, S3]. It computes the entry ($t_{near}$) and exit ($t_{far}$) distances for each slab pair and checks for overlap across all three axes [S1, S3]. Unlike general primitive intersection methods, this approach exploits the axis-aligned nature of the box to reduce the problem to simple scalar comparisons and vector operations, making it the dominant technique for bounding volume hierarchy traversal in ray tracing [S2, unofficial][S3].
