 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Zero Direction Components:** The algorithm produces correct results for rays with zero direction components (parallel to axes) provided the hardware is IEEE floating-point compliant [S2, unofficial][S3]. The division by zero in `inv_ray_dir` results in infinity, which is handled correctly by the min/max logic [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Ray Origin on Box Face:** If the ray origin lies exactly on the box surface and the direction points along the face, the computation may result in `NaN` (Not a Number), causing the test to return `false` [S2, unofficial][S3]. This behavior can be modified if specific handling is required [S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Branchless Optimization:** Removing early exit checks (like `if t_near > t_far`) creates a branchless implementation. While this performs more arithmetic operations, it significantly increases performance on modern hardware by avoiding branch mispredictions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Zero Direction Components:** The algorithm produces correct results for rays with zero direction components (parallel to axes) provided the hardware is IEEE floating-point compliant [S3, unofficial][S4]. The division by zero in `inv_ray_dir` results in infinity, which is handled correctly by the min/max logic [S3, unofficial][S4].
+*   **Ray Origin on Box Face:** If the ray origin lies exactly on the box surface and the direction points along the face, the computation may result in `NaN` (Not a Number), causing the test to return `false` [S3, unofficial][S4]. This behavior can be modified if specific handling is required [S3, unofficial][S4].
